ence of a Limit Using One-Sided Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078651 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078651 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078651 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5f643ade-7715-5788-91bb-d00dfcf9badd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078963 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078963 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d9/d9e89a9b811bdc6b766b01eecf817df48968e3ea604b89bc55bc9660331194e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n1. $lim_(x → - 1)f(x) = 4$\n2. $lim_(x → 1)f(x) = DNE$\n3. $lim_(x → 3)f(x) = 1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91103606-4eee-5423-99c0-f8331e850732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079260 #edb/ref 1061 11988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079260 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e52ecac1-55d5-5dc3-a7ce-c1a112c5ca79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079260 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079420 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079420 #edb/ref 1061 275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079420 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a826f64-60af-5170-b888-9d2ab6f1a053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079420 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079421 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079421 #edb/ref 1061 276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079421 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9edf9846-edef-5bf5-82af-f48490ca2717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079421 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079422 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079422 #edb/ref 1061 279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079422 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "428ef65d-b5b3-5155-832b-8961d7b2d2fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079422 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079615 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the limit of a function $f(x)$ at a point only exists if *both* the left-sided and right-sided limits *exist* and are the *same*.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)$ for the function whose graph is shown below. To do this, we must first compute the left and right-sided limits.\n\n![](images/d9/d99cb9753d63d0812245159b6727e30408eb6cd89d01ecb2f4090f65925527f1.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, $y$ approaches $0$. However, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, $y$ approaches $1$. Therefore, we have the following left and right-sided limits.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Left-sided limit: & lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) = 0 \\\\ Right-sided limit: & lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBoth of these limits exist. However, they are *not* the same. Consequently, the overall limit at $x=2$ does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079615 #edb/ref 1065 147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079615 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5cb3c51e-4afc-5b0f-9559-908488362a25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079615 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
